:: Gather Info [Tutorial] {"position":"2500,2200","size":"100,100"}
<<set $journalNote1 = true>>
<<set $journalNoteTaken = true>>
<<set $noteCount += 1>>
<h1>INT. MS MUNSON'S LIVING ROOM, MORNING</h1>

<h2>Ms Munson</h2>
<i>Ms Munson suddenly stands with her head held high.</i>

I procure, exhibit, and market exquisite shells found within the city right at the borders of our great country! I don't make much, but I love me job. The backyard is where I store me shells 'til the next business day.

<b><i>Journal Updated...</i></b>

<<include "Investigation Options">>

/*

ON ASSIGNING VALUES

Notice how we're doing something with the variables from StoryInit.

"=" is used to assign a value to a variable.
Which means what you assign will overwrite the variable's existing value.

If journalNote5 was set to false before (which we did in StoryInit)...
... then we're overwriting its value here by setting it to true (which we want).

So the next time you open the "Journal" passage, you'll find some new information about backyard access waiting for you. (Because we set journalNote5 to true.)

ON COUNTERS

If you want to keep count of something, a Counter can help with that.
noteCount here is the Counter.

Let's say there are 5 pieces of information you must collect...
...spread between 5 passages.

In which case, each of those passages must have the "noteCount += x" formula.
"+=" just lets you add the value on the right (x) to the noteCount's existing value.

*/


:: Investigate Backyard {"position":"2900,1800","size":"100,100"}
<h2>EXT. MS MUNSON'S BACKYARD, MORNING</h2>

<<if $shellCount >= 3>><i>No gophers here. Oh well. You should take the collected shells back to her.</i>
<<else>><i>You find yourself in Ms Munson's backyard. It's quite small, squared to fit mostly seaweed plants. You see them planted in three rows, each plant decorated with a single seashell.</i>
<</if>>

[[Check the first row.|First Row]]
[[Check the second row.|Second Row]]
[[Check the third row.|Third Row]]
[[Go back...|Investigation Options]]


:: Investigation Options [Tutorial] {"position":"2700,2000","size":"100,100"}
<i>Ms Munson stands in front in you, sobbing.</i>

> "[[Why do you have seashells, may I ask?|Gather Info]]"
> "[[Madame... gophers don't live in the waters.|Contradiction]]"
> "[[Once we finish this interview, may I inspect your backyard?|Request Access to Backyard]]"
> <<if $shellCount >= 3>>"[[Ms Munson, here are your shells.|Deliver Shells]]"<</if>>

<<if $journalNoteTaken == true>>
<hr>
<ul>
<li>[[Check Journal Notes...|Journal]]</li>
<<if $noteCount >= 3>><li>[[Investigate Backyard]]</li><</if>>
</ul>
<</if>>

/*

ON CONDITIONS

The Flags you create and store in StoryInit can be referenced in passages. To make them do something depending on its condition, you use the "if" code.

You can learn more about if-statements online. Just be mindful of two things:

1. close the condition with "/if" by the end of the statement.
2. don't confuse "=" with "= =".

*/


:: Journal {"position":"2900,2000","size":"100,100"}
<h1>MY JOURNAL</h1>

<ul>
<<if $journalNote1 == true>>
	<li>Ms Munson is a shellsperson; she sells seashells near the seashore.</li>
<</if>>

<<if $journalNote4 == true>>
	<li>The gopher has gills. But if a gopher has a nose, and gills are basically noses for fish, then what would the gopher breathe through?</li>
<</if>>
    
<<if $journalNote5 == true>>
	<li>The backyard is open to me to investigate. I'll proceed once I get all my remaining queries answered.</li>
<</if>>
</ul>

<hr>

<nobr><ul>
<<if $noteCount >= 3>>
<li>[[Investigate Backyard]]</li>
<</if>>
<li>[[Go back|Investigation Options]]</li>
</ul></nobr>


