<</if>>

[[Go back...|Investigate Backyard]]


:: Speak to Munson [Tutorial] {"position":"2700,1800","size":"100,100"}
<h1>INT. MS MUNSON'S LIVING ROOM, MORNING</h1>

<h2>Ms Munson</h2>
I woke up early this morning to step out into me backyard, an' I saw the crabbin' gopher, it's out to get me again! I need me seashells! 3 of 'em to gift to me ma and pa!

<<include "Investigation Options">>

/*

ON INCLUDE:

Let's assume you have a few passages where you want your player options to reappear.

The way it works is for every passage where you want those same options to appear...
... you must paste those options there.

For a bite-sized story with a couple of passages, it's not a big deal.
But when you're managing 100+ passages, your sanity will beg you for a merciful death.

Instead, use the "<<include>>" tag (if you're using SugarCube 2)...
...or the "display:" macro (if you're using Harlowe).

All you have to do is pop your player options into a passage...
...and type this into all the passages where you want the options to appear:

<<include "name-of-your-options-passage">>

*/


:: Start Quest [Tutorial] {"position":"2700,1600","size":"100,100"}
<h1>INT. MS MUNSON'S LIVING ROOM, MORNING</h1>

<h2>Ms Munson</h2>Me seashells! I need me seashells! But that gopher won't let me through!

"[[Calm down, Ms Munson. What gopher?|Speak to Munson]]"

/*

ON HTML

Twine's editor recognizes HTML syntax. Ever seen or written HTML code before? Then you'll recognize the h1 and h2 code. They're headers; it's why the text in Play Mode appear so big. If you know HTML basics, you can do a whole lot more with text.


ON PLAYER OPTIONS

In Twine, the "]]" and "[[" signs are used to denote the options players can select in the story.

Just pop in your text between them...
...and that turns into the player option and the passage name.

You'll also notice a separator "|" between the words.

The text before the separator ("Calm down, Ms Munson. What gopher?")...
...is the text the players are going to read.

And the text after the separator ("Speak to Munson")...
...is what the name of the passage the option takes you to is. 

Sometimes, you'll write player-facing text that's too long as a passage name.
The separator helps solve that problem.

*/


:: Seashell Case Init [Tutorial] {"position":"2700,1400","size":"100,100"}
<!-- Flags -->
<<set $journalNote1 = false>>
<<set $journalNote3 = false>>
<<set $journalNote4 = false>>
<<set $journalNote5 = false>>
<<set $journalNoteTaken = false>>

<<set $s1 = false>>
<<set $s2 = false>>
<<set $s3 = false>>

<!-- Counters -->
<<set $noteCount = 0>>
<<set $shellCount = 0>>

/*

This is your StoryInit.

(In the Atlantis build this passage is "Seashell Case Init". The real StoryInit in story.twee pulls it in with <<include "Seashell Case Init">>, so the quest keeps its variables in its own file.)

It's basically where all the story-wide variables live.

What you see above are all variables.
There are many other types of variables, but these two are worth learning for now:

Flags (stuff that go true or false)...
...and Counters (stuff that keep count of an action).

If it starts with a "$" sign, it can be accessed anywhere by any passage in this .twee file.

A variable that lives and dies in just one specific passage starts with an "_". These usually are found in the passage using it, not the StoryInit passage.

*/


:: Third Row {"position":"3100,2000","size":"100,100"}
<h2>EXT. MS MUNSON'S BACKYARD, MORNING</h2>

<i>You check the third row of plants. All except one have a seashell pinned on them.</i>

<<if $s2 == false>>
	<<link "Pick a random seashell." "SeashellPlusPlus">>
    <<set $s2 = true>>
    <</link>>
<</if>>
<<if $s3 == false>>
	<<link "Check the ground near the plant without a seashell." "SeashellPlusPlus">>
    <<set $s3 = true>>
    <</link>>
<</if>>

[[Go back...|Investigate Backyard]]
